## Why This Is Asked
Real-world cache invalidation across services affects consistency and latency; this tests practical patterns for multi-cache coherence.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside with per-key versioning
- Event-driven invalidation via a bus
- Redis vs Memcached nuances (pub/sub, TTLs, atomic ops)
- Thundering herd mitigation and stale-while-revalidate

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo
function write(key, value){
  db.update(key, value);
  publisher.publish('invalidate', {key, version: newVersion(key)});
}
function read(key){
  const v = cache.getVersion(key);
  const val = cache.get(key);
  if (val && val.version === v) return val.value;
  const fresh = fetchFromDB(key);
  cache.set(key, {value: fresh, version: v+1}, ttl);
  return fresh;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle drift between Redis and Memcached?
- How would you test invalidation at scale?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes how to architect a multi-IdP authentication layer that scales across tenants, focusing on correctness and operational concerns rather than theory.

## Key Concepts

- OIDC token validation (audience, issuer, nonce) and SAML assertion validation (Signature, Conditions, AudienceRestriction)
- JWKS rotation and per-tenant metadata caching
- JWT binding with per-device nonce and PKCE for public clients
- Refresh token rotation and reuse detection
- Scalable, cross-region revocation and token introspection

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal token issuance example
function issueJwt(payload, kid) {
  const header = { alg: 'RS256', kid };
  const token = jwt.sign(payload, getPrivateKeyForKid(kid), {
    algorithm: 'RS256',
    header,
    expiresIn: '15m'
  });
  return token;
}
```

