## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design practical cache invalidation and explain trade-offs of Redis vs Memcached in a beginner context.

## Key Concepts

- cache-aside pattern
- tenant isolation in keys
- stampede mitigation with locks
- event-bus invalidation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode illustrating lazy reload with lock
async function getProduct(tid, pid) {
  const key = `tenant:${tid}:product:${pid}`
  let val = cache.get(key)
  if (val) return val
  const lock = `lock:${tid}:${pid}`
  if (redis.set(lock, 1, 'NX', 'PX', 1000)) {
    val = db.fetchProduct(tid, pid)
    cache.set(key, val, 300)
    redis.del(lock)
  } else {
    await sleep(50)
    return getProduct(tid, pid)
  }
  return val
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this under load?
- How would you handle cross-tenant data leakage concerns?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design distributed workflow with real systems concepts: saga orchestration, event sourcing, outbox pattern, and idempotent handlers under failure. Focuses on practical correctness and maintainability at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration across multiple services
- Event sourcing and outbox integration
- Idempotent handlers using correlationId and event.id
- Compensating actions for partial failures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo orchestrator sketch
function onEvent(evt) {
  const key = `${evt.correlationId}:${evt.id}`;
  if (dedupe(key)) return;
  markHandled(key);
  switch (evt.type) {
    case 'OrderPlaced':
      enqueue('ReserveInventory', { orderId: evt.orderId, items: evt.items }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
    case 'InventoryReserved':
      enqueue('AuthorizePayment', { orderId: evt.orderId, amount: evt.total }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
    case 'PaymentAuthorized':
      enqueue('ShipOrder', { orderId: evt.orderId }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
    case 'OrderFailed':
      enqueue('ReleaseInventory', { orderId: evt.orderId }, evt.correlationId);
      enqueue('RefundPayment', { orderId: evt.orderId }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of basic sharding and replication in a realistic chat system. Emphasizes data locality, tenant isolation, and safe rebalancing with minimal downtime.

## Key Concepts

- Shard key choice: user_id for even distribution
- Replication factor: 3 for durability
- Hot-partition handling: detect and migrate
- Rebalancing: rolling data moves
- Read path with tenant checks: enforce isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo JS shard map
class ShardMap {
  constructor(size) {
    this.size = size;
    this.map = new Map();
  }
  getShard(userId) {
    const idx = userId % this.size;
    return this.map.get(idx) || idx;
  }
}
```

